45
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:57,000
So joining us today on the Scout Master Podcast are three members of the National Advancement Committee.

46
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Steve Bowen is out in El Centro, California, and Steve is Area 6

47
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
or Western Region Program Support Chairman.

48
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
He's also a member of the National Program Content Committee.

49
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:17,000
He is a team leader for the Merit Badge Advancement Activities for the upcoming 2013 National Jamboree.

50
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
He's also earned this Distinguished Eagle Scout Award and the Silver Buffalo.

51
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
How are you doing this evening, Steve?

52
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Very fine. thank you, Clark.

53
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:36,000
And Wayne Huddleson is in Omaha, Nebraska, where he is a National Advancement Committee Eagle Rank and Palms Specialist.

54
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
He's also a Silver Beaver recipient, and Wayne was involved in something many of you have seen,

55
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:51,000
and that was the Union Pacific Railroad Locomotive that was set up and had the Special Paint Scheme for the Centenary.

56
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Wayne was part of making that happen,

57
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
So we owe you some thanks for that, Wayne. How are you doing?

58
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Glad to be here, Clark.

59
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Finally, Dan Maxfield is in Fresno, California, where he's the Council Vice President for District Operations,

60
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
the Area Vice President for Council Support on the National Commissioner Support Staff.

61
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Dan was also part of the editorial team that produced the 2010 Centenary Boy Scout Handbook

62
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
and he's also a Silver Antelope recipient.

63
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
So good evening, Dan. How are you?

64
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Great tonight and glad to be here with you, Clark.

65
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Well, I want to say I really appreciate you guys taking the time to do this as a part of what you do for scouting,

66
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:44,000
And the reason I asked you to be here was to give scout leaders an idea of how things like this new Guide to Advancement happen.

67
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:53,000
This fall, the BSA released a significant new resource for us volunteers, and that is that Guide to Advancement

68
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,000
replaces the publication that used to be called the Advancement Committee Policies and Procedures Manual,

69
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
and it features some significant changes and clarifications.

70
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,000
One of the things I'm really interested in is how things like this happen.

71
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
How do you end up on the National Advancement Team?

72
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Well, this is Steve. I'll start off.

73
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
The previous Advancement Committee was one of many national committees

74
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and the new Advancement Committee has actually kind of evolved back into a full-time committee

75
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
because Advancement is something that goes on all the time.

76
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:35,000
But people were asked to be on that committee who had backgrounds in different special areas,

77
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
maybe some previous Advancement Committee work,

78
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:46,000
but generally they were selected from across the nation for their different expertise in different fields.

79
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,000
So this team gets together and then what kind of information or statistics or input do you use

80
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
to put together something like the Guide to Advancement?

81
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Well, this is, Dan, The process that the National BSA uses now on anything like this.

82
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:14,000
the way they're developed is through a committee structure that includes a lot of folks from around the country.

83
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:21,000
At any given time that could be 50 to 100 folks- and those folks are surveyed through email

84
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and phone conversations. data is collected, it's collated

85
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:33,000
and then a smaller group of folks will take all that data, formulate something that says:

86
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
well, we think this is maybe what's the result of that data,

87
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and then put that back out to all of those folks and say: is this what you told us?

88
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Get some input, refine and develop that down to something that then everybody tends to buy in

89
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,000
or agree on after some discussion and interaction over time

90
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
and then that becomes the product.

91
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:06,000
But I should point out here that the material is written by a small group or a single individual,

92
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
and in the case of the Guide to Advancement, by a single individual,

93
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:17,000
simply because there's a need for a continuity of voice and content,

94
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:24,000
and a committee trying to write something like that would not have that characteristic.

95
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,000
so typically something like this today is being written by one individual,

96
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:34,000
with constant guidance and interaction from a group of folks.

97
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:40,000
You're soliciting opinions and information from volunteers like me.

98
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,000
You betcha,

99
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
It's not done in a vacuum. in other words,

100
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
No. in the case of the Guide to Advancement,

101
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
there were over 50 volunteers around the country that gave ongoing input,

102
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:59,000
plus about 50 professionals that gave feedback into this whole process as well.

103
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:04,000
but it's important to understand once a viable draft is developed.

104
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
this goes to health and safety, it goes to special needs,

105
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
it goes to legal and all the different departments within the BSA

106
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
to make sure that all of the opponents of the Boy Scouts of America

107
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:23,000
are in accord and in agreement, so that there's a one voice coming out of this thing.

108
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Clark, I'd like to add something.

109
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:25,000
This is Steve

110
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Over the years and Dan and I were on that Old Advancement Committee

111
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:34,000
for about 15 years apiece. a lot of things came up repeatedly

112
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
The same problem in Eagle Appeals or a Board of Review or something like that.

113
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,000
A log was kept that picked up these problems

114
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:49,000
and that became another resource for developing this new guide

115
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
to cover all those little issues that just kept being problems.

116
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
That actually comes from the field.

117
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:59,000
In our discussion before we started, you guys mentioned too

118
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,000
that committee also answers Eagle Appeals and things like that.

119
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
so you're dealing with direct things on an ongoing basis.

120
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Absolutely correct,

121
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
This is Wayne.

122
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
One of the things that you see and you've mentioned in some of your other podcasts

123
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:20,000
is some of the changes. things to the project forms and things like that

124
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:24,000
were in response to some of the problems that Steve's talking about,

125
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
where he was taking months to get approval for a project

126
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
or things like that. that seemed excessive.

127
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
You see some clarification in the guide now in those areas.

128
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
This is Dan.

129
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
I would add to that as well that we teach classes at FilmWant

130
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
during the summer and we teach classes at the sea base

131
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
during the January timeframe.

132
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
That's a little commercial right there

133
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
in case anybody's interested in signing up for those,

134
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
But at those classes we interact directly face-to-face

135
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
with volunteers from the field and they tell us this stuff

136
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
and we kept this information from them

137
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
and we have a lot of face-to-face discussions

138
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
that give us a good sense of what's out there in the field

139
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
and how do they feel about it.

140
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,000
About how long did this whole process take, would you guess?

141
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
I'd say it took about a year.

142
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Do you guys agree with that?

143
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
This is Steve.

144
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:20,000
There were some changes to the guide in 2009,

145
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
but they just weren't what was necessary,

146
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and so a whole new guide was developed.

147
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Chris Hunt at the National Office

148
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
has really been the torch bearer for this.

149
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
I would say, like Dan says, a year,

150
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
maybe probably two years, if you think all the time

151
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
that was kind of built in re-evaluating the previous document.

152
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:47,000
It was a full, solid year of direct writing and interaction.

153
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
We had a preliminary draft in January,

154
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
so actually a lot of work had gone on before then.

155
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
How did you resolve debates or questions

156
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
that may have been a bit of a problem

157
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
or there were different opinions.

158
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:09,000
The thing is that when divergences of opinions would come in-

159
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and many cases Chris Hunt, whom Steve alluded to a moment ago,

160
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
would simply call those people directly and say:

161
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
look, here's what you said, why,

162
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
and would get their input and interaction

163
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
and would give him the feedback from some of the rest of us

164
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
that he was hearing and say:

165
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
well, look, this is what I'm hearing from others.

166
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Are you in agreement here

167
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
or is there a major disagreement? or can we resolve this?

168
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:38,000
And more often than not, most of it was resolved

169
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,000
simply that way.

170
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
One or two things were fairly contentious

171
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and I think eventually Chris had to simply say

172
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
the majority believed this. so that's what I'm going with.

173
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
This is Wayne, and one thing to keep in mind

174
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
is it's intended that this document's a living document.

175
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Another reason for it being on the internet

176
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
is you can see this thing change

177
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
or be updated as conditions warrant.

178
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
New information comes in, that gets considered

179
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:04,000
and things like that.

180
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:07,000
We don't anticipate going 10 years before changing it again.

181
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
I guess, with the way that the internet

182
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
has enabled a lot of this stuff,

183
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,000
it's easier to get input now,

184
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
it's easier to make the changes,

185
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
it's easier to publish the changes.

186
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
The downside of that is, like you say,

187
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,000
it's easier to get input,

188
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
and the problem is sometimes you get so much of it,

189
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,000
you become overburdened,

190
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
and that was some of the problems that the group faced too

191
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
was trying to deal with so many opinions

192
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and so many places at the same time.

193
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,000
The one thing that I absolutely was thrilled with

194
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
as I sat and read the new guide

195
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,000
was the emphasis of a proportional approach

196
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,000
to advancement as one method of many in scouting.

197
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
All of us have known for a long, long time

198
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:54,000
that advancement is just one of the eight methods

199
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
of Boy Scout program development.

200
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
Advancement is the one that everybody sees, I think,

201
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
but there are so many other kind of subtle things

202
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,000
which are part of the methods of scouting.

203
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,000
So this is not the whole nutshell,

204
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
this is just a portion of it,

205
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
And this is Dan.

206
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
I would point out that I absolutely echo

207
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
what Steve just said.

208
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
but there was clearly a recognition

209
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
on the part of the folk that put together

210
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
the current Journey to Excellent program

211
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:29,000
that we're now on,

212
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
which to the point that it became a specific item

213
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
in the Journey to Excellence criterion,

214
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,000
because it's very clear over studies over time

215
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
that when kids in a unit-

216
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
whether it's Cub Scouts, Boy Scouts, Venturing,

217
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
it doesn't matter- are having fun

218
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:50,000
and they're learning something,

219
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
they tend to stay longer in the program,

220
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
and ultimately that is our goal, isn't it?

221
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
The most problematic part of this sometimes

222
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
is just getting it right,

223
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
is getting that right balance:

224
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
being able to run an advancement program

225
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:10,000
and have all the other things happening at the same time

226
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
without over-emphasizing one or the other.

227
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
This is Ryan. let me build a little bit on that.

228
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
One of the things I want to point out is,

229
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
if we think back about what we do in scouting,

230
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
what we want from scouting,

231
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
we're really looking for excellence in character,

232
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
citizenship and fitness.

233
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Even though they're earning these awards,

234
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
what we're really hoping for

235
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
is a lot of time of excellence

236
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
by living by the Scouts of St Louis.

237
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
I think the guide is really going to help us

238
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
reach those goals

239
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
and I think it really helps.

240
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
it really lays a very clear path

241
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
on how to administer the advancement part of the program.

242
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
The kids don't know

243
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
